# Retired Loom payload files — target-relative paths (in the same
# report-relative form resync-installed.sh's own per-file report and
# .loom/resync-ignore use, e.g. "scripts/status.sh", "roles/foo.md",
# "hooks/bar.sh", "docs/baz.md", "bin/loom", "commands/loom/qux.md",
# ".claude/README.md", ".github/CONFIGURATION.md") that USED TO ship
# under defaults/ but have since been deleted there.
#
# resync-installed.sh (`remove_retired_files`) reads this file every run and
# removes any listed path still present in an already-installed repo,
# reporting it with the "removed" verb — this is the delete-side counterpart
# to the update/create walk over defaults/ itself, which can only ever see
# files that STILL exist upstream and therefore never notices a retirement
# (#5981).
#
# Format: one entry per line. Lines starting with "#" and blank lines are
# ignored; trailing "# comment" text and surrounding whitespace are
# stripped. Exact-string match only — no globbing.
#
# A consumer that deliberately wants to keep a retired file (e.g. a local
# fork of it) can pin it in their own .loom/resync-ignore using the exact
# same entry text — the removal honors that pin exactly like an update
# would.
#
# NEVER remove an entry from this list once added: an already-installed
# repo that hasn't resynced in a while must still catch up on every
# retirement since its last run, not just the most recent one.

# #5710 / #5718 — orphaned agent-status-file protocol (report/get/list/clear);
# agent state is daemon-owned now (sweep registry, heartbeat, event bus) and
# nothing calls it. Added #5981 so the CHANGELOG's original removal claim
# actually holds on the next resync.
scripts/status.sh

# #6628 — Tier 2 dispatch toolchain validator (loom-cleanup /
# loom-recover-orphans presence check) with zero in-tree callers: no CI
# workflow, role doc, or script invokes it. The thin root scripts/
# validate-toolchain.sh wrapper that exec'd this file is removed in the same
# change (repo-only, not a defaults/ payload — not listed here).
scripts/validate-toolchain.sh
